<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Grave Circle A</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1600–1500 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century-bce">17th century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Grave Circle A is a royal cemetery just south of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lion-gate">Lion Gate</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mycenae">Mycenae</a> in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a>, a circle 27.5 metres across with six shaft graves used from about 1600 to 1500 BCE. Its nineteen burials of men, women and children held weapons, jewellery, gold and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> cups and gold death masks, among them the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mask-of-agamemnon">Mask of Agamemnon</a>, with an estimated 15 kilograms of gold in all. First outside the walls, it was enclosed in the citadel when the fortifications were extended about 1250 BCE. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heinrich-schliemann">Heinrich Schliemann</a> and Panagiotis Stamatakis excavated it in 1876–1877.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/grave-circle-a.jpg" alt="Grave Circle A" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Dennis G. Jarvis · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Greece-0388_(2215117229).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
